{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And the men are shepherds.</b> That is to say, their own; and lest you understand that the flocks belonged to others and they worked for hire, therefore he added to say that they were men of livestock, to indicate that they did not belong to others but to themselves, for they were very wealthy. And the reason the tribes chose this craft, which was the craft of their fathers, is because of two things: the first, that there is great profit in it — in fleece and fat and offspring — and it is a craft that does not require great exertion and much toil, and is without sin; and concerning it Solomon warned in his wisdom (Proverbs 27:23): ״ידע תדע פני צאנך שית לבך לעדרים״. And the second, because the tribes knew that they and their seed would be exiled to Egypt, and since the Egyptians worshipped the form of the sheep, they took for themselves this craft so that their seed would be accustomed to it, and the worship of the sheep would be far from their eyes. And so you will find that most of the righteous and the prophets were shepherds: you find it in Hevel (Genesis 4:2): ״ויהי הבל רועה צאן״; and likewise in Moshe (Exodus 3:1): ״ומשה היה רועה״; and likewise Samuel the prophet, and likewise Sha'ul and David — all of them shepherds. And the reason in their case is so that they would distance themselves from settled areas, because many transgressions follow from association with people, such as gossip and slander and false oaths and uncovering of nakedness and robbery and violence; and whatever a man withdraws from the company of creatures, he escapes from transgressions, and whatever he distances himself from settlement, he distances himself from sin and from robbery and violence and the rest of the transgressions. And the proof is what is written of Lot (Genesis 19:30): ״ויעל לוט מצוער וישב בהר״ — he needed to leave Zoar lest he be swept away in their iniquity; and after he left there he went to Zoar to find refuge there, as he said (ibid.): ״הנה נא העיר הזאת קרובה לנוס שמה והיא מצער״ — because its inhabitants were recent arrivals, therefore their iniquities were few (מצערין); and even so he was still afraid in Zoar because of the dread of the iniquities found in settlement, therefore he needed to leave there for the mountain; and furthermore, there is the place of seclusion for prophecy, and no man sees anything there that would disturb his thought from meditation upon HaShem, may He be exalted." "<b>And you already</b> know that among the greatest of the prophets there were those who would withdraw and separate from settlement and seclude themselves in the wilderness even without a pasture — and they are Eliyahu and Elisha. Thus it is written of Elisha (I Kings 19:19): ״ושנים עשר צמדים לפניו״, and he separated from this craft and went after him, as it is written (I Kings ibid.): ״אלכה נא אשקה את אבי ואת אמי ואלכה אחריך״, and it is written (ibid.): ״וילך אחרי אליהו וישרתהו״. And likewise it was undoubtedly the practice of the sons of the prophets in that generation and the one before it to leave the affairs of the world and its settlement, and to leave providence over matters of the body, and to go out to the wildernesses to seclude themselves there and to have their thought cleave to HaShem, may He be exalted."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:57.845340072Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}